Lithuanian law contains no offence of hitchhiking (''autostopas''), and on ordinary roads it is legal. [https://e-seimas.lrs.lt/rs/legalact/TAD/2a948a80506e11e485f39f55fd139d01/ Item 174 of the ''Kelių eismo taisyklės''] lists what is forbidden on a road signed ''Automagistralė'' or ''Automobilių kelias'', and 174.1 begins with pedestrians — no walking, no animals, no bicycles or mopeds, nothing but cars, motorcycles and the like capable of at least 60 km/h. Item 174.2 forbids stopping or standing except in the specially designated areas, 174.3 bans U-turns and reversing, and 174.4 allows joining and leaving only at the places provided, so the slip road is closed to you as well as the carriageway. The designated ''aikštelės'' and their petrol stations are therefore the only spot on those roads where a lift can lawfully begin. Everywhere else — the ''krašto'' and ''rajoniniai keliai'' that carry most of the traffic — thumbing from the verge breaks no rule.
